Pass ier to inputerror from hcfs.f, as every other caller does.

CalculiX 2.22 declares

    subroutine inputerror(inpc,ipoinpc,iline,text,ier)

and 657 of its 660 call sites pass all five arguments. The three in hcfs.f stop
after the text. In native Fortran that reads an undefined ier, which happens to
be harmless because inputerror only ever sets it. Under f2c on wasm the missing
argument is a hole in the call: at wasm32 every argument was an i32 and the
slot was filled with whatever was on the stack, unnoticed; at wasm64 the caller
and the definition no longer agree --

    wasm-ld: warning: function signature mismatch: inputerror_
    >>> defined as (i64, i64, i64, i64, i32, i32, i64) -> void in libccx.a(f_hcfs.o)
    >>> defined as (i64, i64, i64, i64, i64, i32, i32) -> void in libccx.a(f_inputerror.o)

-- and wasm-ld routes the call through a stub that traps, so a malformed *HCF
card would trap the solver instead of printing the error. ier is in scope in
hcfs (it is the subroutine's own argument).

--- a/src/hcfs.f
+++ b/src/hcfs.f
@@ -94,7 +94,7 @@
         write(*,*) '         ',
      &       textpart(i)(1:index(textpart(i),' ')-1)
         call inputerror(inpc,ipoinpc,iline,
-     &       "*HCF%")
+     &       "*HCF%",ier)
       endif
 !     
       if(mei(1).eq.0) then
@@ -103,7 +103,7 @@
         write(*,*) '         ',
      &       textpart(i)(1:index(textpart(i),' ')-1)
         call inputerror(inpc,ipoinpc,iline,
-     &       "*HCF%")
+     &       "*HCF%",ier)
       endif
 !     
       if(mei(2).eq.0) then
@@ -112,7 +112,7 @@
         write(*,*) '         ',
      &       textpart(i)(1:index(textpart(i),' ')-1)
         call inputerror(inpc,ipoinpc,iline,
-     &       "*HCF%")
+     &       "*HCF%",ier)
       endif
 !     
       if(tincf.eq.0.d0) then
